S = []
def s(id, title, t, refs, emoji, a, b, c, talk, mem=None, hue=200):
    S.append(dict(id=id, title=title, testament=t, refs=refs, emoji=emoji, text={'4-6': a, '7-9': b, '10-12': c}, talk=talk, memory=mem, hue=hue))

s('creation','God Makes the World','OT',['Genesis 1:1-2:3'],'🌍',
 "In the very beginning there was nothing. Then God spoke, and light shone! God made the sky and the sea. He made the land, with trees and flowers. He made the sun, the moon and the stars. He made fish and birds and animals. Last of all, God made people, to be his children. God looked at everything and said, 'It is very good!' On the seventh day God rested.",
 "Long ago, before anything else, there was God. God said, 'Let there be light,' and there was light! Day by day, God made the world. He made the sky, the oceans and the dry land. He made plants and trees, the sun, moon and stars, fish, birds and animals. On the sixth day God made people, a man and a woman, to look after the world and to know and love him. Everything God made was good, and people were very good. On the seventh day God rested and made the day special.",
 "The Bible begins with God, who was there before everything. By his word alone he brings order out of emptiness: light, sky, sea and land, then plants, the sun, moon and stars, then creatures of the water, air and earth. Each time, God sees that it is good. People are the last and most special part: made 'in the image of God', able to know and love him, and trusted to take care of creation. God rests on the seventh day, which is why Sunday, the Lord's day, is a day for rest and for worship. The story is not a science lesson. It tells us who made us and why: because God is good, and the world is a gift.",
 ['What is your favourite thing God made?','What does it mean to take care of what God made?','Why do you think God rested on the seventh day?'],'Genesis 1:31',hue=140)

s('adam-eve','Adam, Eve and the Garden','OT',['Genesis 2:4-3:24'],'🍎',
 "God made a beautiful garden, and put Adam and Eve in it. They had everything they needed. God said, 'You may eat from every tree, except one.' But a sly snake said, 'Eat it! God is not telling you the truth.' Eve and Adam ate the fruit. They broke God's rule, and they felt afraid. They hid from God. God was sad, but he still loved them. He promised that one day someone would come to put things right.",
 "God made a garden called Eden for the first man and woman, Adam and Eve. They walked and talked with God, and had everything they needed. God gave them one rule: do not eat the fruit of the tree in the middle of the garden. A sly serpent tricked Eve, saying that God was keeping something good from them. Eve ate the fruit and gave some to Adam. At once they felt ashamed and hid. God called, 'Where are you?' They had to leave the garden. But God did not stop loving them. He promised that someone from Eve's family would one day defeat the serpent. That someone is Jesus.",
 "In the garden of Eden, Adam and Eve lived close to God. God gave one boundary: they must not eat from the tree of the knowledge of good and evil. The serpent whispered a lie, that God could not be trusted and that they could become like God. They chose to disobey, and the first thing sin did was break trust: they hid, blamed each other and were sent out of the garden. We call this the Fall. But notice that God goes looking for them ('Where are you?') and makes them clothes. He also promises that the woman's offspring will crush the serpent's head. The Church has always seen in this first promise the hint of Jesus and of Mary, the new Eve.",
 ['Why did Adam and Eve hide?','When have you done something wrong and wanted to hide? What helped?','How did God show he still loved them?'],'Genesis 3:9',hue=15)

s('cain-abel','Cain and Abel','OT',['Genesis 4:1-16'],'🌾',
 "Adam and Eve had two sons, Cain and Abel. Abel looked after sheep. Cain grew food. Both brought gifts to God. God was pleased with Abel's gift, and Cain felt angry and jealous. God said, 'Be careful, Cain. Do what is good.' But Cain did not listen. He hurt his brother. This made God very sad. We must never be jealous or hurt others. We should be kind.",
 "Cain and Abel were Adam and Eve's sons. Cain was a farmer and Abel was a shepherd. Each brought God an offering. God was pleased with Abel's gift, which came from the best he had. Cain grew angry and jealous. God warned him, 'Sin is waiting at your door, but you can master it.' Cain did not listen. He led Abel into a field and killed him. When God asked, 'Where is your brother?' Cain answered, 'Am I my brother's keeper?' The answer is yes: we are all meant to look after each other. Cain had to leave his home, but God still marked him with protection.",
 "This is the first story of sin spreading from a heart to a family. Abel offers the best of his flock; Cain's offering is received with less favour, and jealousy grows into anger. God speaks to Cain before it is too late, saying sin is crouching at the door but he can rule over it. Cain chooses violence anyway, and then lies: 'Am I my brother's keeper?' God hears Abel's blood crying out from the ground. Even now God protects Cain from revenge, showing that mercy is stronger than vengeance. The Church remembers Abel as the first just man, and the Letter to the Hebrews says his faith still speaks.",
 ['Why was Cain jealous?','What could Cain have done instead?','How can we be our brother’s or sister’s keeper today?'],'Genesis 4:9',hue=35)

s('noah','Noah and the Ark','OT',['Genesis 6:5-9:17'],'🌈',
 "People stopped listening to God. But Noah loved God and did what was right. God said, 'Noah, build a big boat called an ark.' Noah built it. Then he took in his family and two of each kind of animal. Rain fell for many days. The ark floated safely. When the water went down, Noah sent out a dove. It came back with an olive leaf. God put a rainbow in the sky. It is a sign of his promise.",
 "God saw that the world had filled with violence and sadness. But Noah was a good man who walked with God. God told him to build a great ark, a huge boat, and to bring his family and pairs of every animal inside. Noah trusted God and did exactly as he was told, even when others laughed. Then it rained and rained, and water covered the earth. The ark floated for many months. At last the water went down, and Noah sent out a dove. It came back with an olive leaf. When they stepped onto dry land, God promised never to flood the whole earth again and set his rainbow in the clouds.",
 "Noah's story shows how serious God is about goodness, and how patient he is with people who trust him. Noah builds the ark for years on the word of God alone, which is what faith looks like: doing what God asks even when you cannot see the end. The flood is a kind of new beginning for creation, and the dove and olive branch have become signs of peace. God's covenant after the flood is with every living creature, with the rainbow as its sign. The Fathers of the Church saw the ark as a picture of the Church, carrying people safely through the waters, and Saint Peter links the flood to Baptism.",
 ['How do you think Noah felt when people laughed?','What does the rainbow remind us of?','What is something God has asked you to trust him about?'],'Genesis 9:13',hue=210)

s('babel','The Tower of Babel','OT',['Genesis 11:1-9'],'🏗️',
 "Long ago everyone spoke the same language. They said, 'Let us build a tall tower to reach the sky. We will make ourselves famous!' But they forgot about God. God saw their proud hearts. He gave them different languages. Now they could not understand each other. So they stopped building, and spread across the earth. We should not be proud. We should thank God for all we have.",
 "In the early days everyone on earth spoke one language. They found a plain and said, 'Let us build a city and a tower that reaches the heavens, and make a name for ourselves.' They were proud, and wanted to be great without God. God came down to look. He mixed up their speech so they could not understand one another. The building stopped and people scattered over the earth. The city was called Babel, which sounds like the Hebrew word for 'mixed up'.",
 "Babel shows pride at its most ambitious: a united humanity using its skill to 'make a name for ourselves' instead of honouring God. God is not threatened by a tower; he is protecting people from the harm that comes when pride has no limits. The confusion of languages scatters them. The story has a beautiful answer in the New Testament. At Pentecost the Holy Spirit comes and people from every nation hear the apostles in their own language. What pride divided, the Spirit brings together in the Church.",
 ['Why did the people want to build the tower?','What happened at Pentecost that reminds us of Babel?','What is the difference between being proud and being thankful?'],'Genesis 11:4',hue=30)

s('abraham-call','God Calls Abraham','OT',['Genesis 12:1-9','Genesis 15:1-6'],'⭐',
 "God spoke to a man named Abraham. 'Leave your home and go to a new land. I will bless you.' Abraham was old, but he trusted God and went. Later God took Abraham outside at night. 'Look at the stars,' God said. 'Can you count them? Your family will be that big!' Abraham believed God.",
 "Abraham, who was called Abram at first, lived in a land far from the one God had in mind. One day God said, 'Leave your country and your family and go to the land I will show you. I will make you a great nation, and through you all families of the earth will be blessed.' Abraham did not know where he was going, but he packed up and went with his wife Sarah. Years later, with no children yet, God took him outside under the night sky and said, 'Count the stars, if you can. That is how many people will come from you.' Abraham believed God.",
 "With Abraham God begins his plan to save the world through one family. The call is simple and hard: leave everything familiar and trust. Abraham is seventy-five and childless, yet God promises a land, countless descendants and a blessing that will reach every nation. 'Abram believed the Lord, and he reckoned it to him as righteousness.' Saint Paul points to this verse again and again: Abraham is the father of all who believe. Every time we trust God without seeing the whole road, we follow him.",
 ['Would you have gone with Abraham? Why?','What do the stars remind us about God’s promises?','When is it hard to trust God?'],'Genesis 15:6',hue=250)

s('isaac','Abraham and Isaac on the Mountain','OT',['Genesis 21:1-7','Genesis 22:1-19'],'🐏',
 "God gave Abraham and Sarah a baby boy named Isaac, and they were so happy! When Isaac grew, God wanted to see how much Abraham trusted him. Abraham and Isaac climbed a mountain together. Isaac asked, 'Where is the lamb?' Abraham said, 'God will provide.' Just in time, an angel called out, 'Stop!' God showed them a ram. God had taken care of everything.",
 "Abraham and Sarah waited many years, and at last God gave them a son, Isaac. He was the answer to a promise. One day God tested Abraham's trust. He asked him to take Isaac up a mountain and offer him to God. Abraham obeyed, though his heart must have been heavy. Isaac carried the wood and asked, 'Where is the lamb?' Abraham said, 'God will provide.' Then, just as Abraham was ready, an angel of the Lord cried out, 'Do not harm the boy!' Abraham looked up and saw a ram caught in the bushes, and offered that instead. God never wanted Isaac to be hurt. He wanted Abraham to know that God can always be trusted.",
 "This is one of the most challenging stories in the Bible, so it is good to read it slowly. God tests Abraham, and Abraham trusts that God is faithful to his promise, even believing, as the Letter to the Hebrews says, that God could raise the dead. At the last moment God stops the sacrifice: he never asks for human sacrifice, and the story teaches that clearly. The ram is provided, 'God will provide.' Christians have long seen a picture of Jesus here: a beloved only son who carries the wood up a hill, and a Father who this time does not spare his Son but gives him for us. The place is called Moriah, later the Temple mount.",
 ['What did Abraham mean when he said, “God will provide”?','Why do you think God stopped Abraham?','How is Isaac carrying the wood like Jesus carrying his cross?'],'Genesis 22:8',hue=25)

s('jacob-esau','Jacob and Esau','OT',['Genesis 25:19-34','Genesis 27:1-45'],'🥣',
 "Isaac and Rebekah had twin boys, Esau and Jacob. Esau was hairy and loved to hunt. Jacob stayed home. One day Esau was very hungry, and Jacob had some stew. Jacob tricked him, and later tricked their father too. This was not the right way to do things. Jacob had to run away. But God still loved Jacob, and did not give up on him.",
 "Isaac and Rebekah had twin sons. Esau, the older, loved to hunt. Jacob was quiet and stayed by the tents. One day Esau came home very hungry and traded his rights as the oldest son for a bowl of Jacob's stew. Later, when old Isaac was nearly blind and ready to give his special blessing to Esau, Jacob dressed up and pretended to be his brother. Isaac blessed Jacob by mistake. Esau was furious, and Jacob had to flee. Jacob had done wrong, but God did not stop loving him. He would be the father of the twelve tribes of Israel.",
 "God keeps choosing flawed people, and Jacob is a good example. His name means 'he grasps the heel', or 'he cheats', and he lives up to it: he trades a bowl of stew for his brother's birthright, then deceives his blind father to steal the blessing. The story does not call this right. Jacob pays a price in years of exile and in being tricked himself. Yet God's promise to Abraham and Isaac continues through him. It is a reminder that God's plan does not depend on how good we are, and that he gently works to change hearts, as he does with Jacob later at the river Jabbok.",
 ['Was it right for Jacob to trick Esau and Isaac?','What happens when we try to get what we want by cheating?','How does God show mercy to Jacob?'],'Genesis 28:15',hue=45)

s('jacob-ladder','Jacob’s Ladder','OT',['Genesis 28:10-22'],'🪜',
 "Jacob was far from home, and he was alone. He put a stone under his head and fell asleep. In a dream he saw a ladder reaching up to heaven. Angels went up and down. God spoke: 'I am with you. I will never leave you.' Jacob woke up and said, 'God is here, and I did not know it!'",
 "Jacob was running away from home, and night fell. He had no bed, so he used a stone for a pillow. In a dream he saw a ladder that reached from earth up to heaven, and God's angels were climbing up and down it. At the top stood the Lord, who said, 'I am with you and will watch over you wherever you go. I will bring you back.' Jacob woke up, amazed. 'Surely the Lord is in this place, and I did not know it!' In the morning he set up the stone as a marker and called the place Bethel, which means 'House of God'.",
 "Jacob is alone, guilty and afraid, and it is exactly there that God meets him. The ladder joins heaven and earth, with angels carrying messages between them, and God repeats the promise made to Abraham and Isaac. He adds something Jacob needs most: 'I am with you.' Jacob calls it the gate of heaven. In John's Gospel Jesus tells Nathanael that he will see 'the angels of God ascending and descending upon the Son of Man': Jesus himself is the ladder, the meeting place of God and humanity. Every Catholic church is a Bethel, a house of God.",
 ['Where have you felt that God was near, even when you did not expect it?','Why did Jacob call the place the gate of heaven?','How is Jesus like the ladder?'],'Genesis 28:15',hue=265)

s('joseph1','Joseph and His Colourful Coat','OT',['Genesis 37:1-36'],'🧥',
 "Jacob loved his son Joseph very much. He gave Joseph a beautiful coat. Joseph's brothers were jealous. Joseph told them his dreams, and that made them more cross. One day, the brothers sold Joseph to travellers who took him to Egypt. Joseph was sad and far from home. But God was still with him.",
 "Jacob had twelve sons, and he loved Joseph best, giving him a special coat of many colours. Joseph also had dreams in which his brothers bowed down to him, and when he told them, they grew angrier. One day, far from their father, the brothers seized Joseph, threw him into a pit and then sold him to traders going to Egypt. They dipped the coat in goat's blood and told their father Joseph was dead. Jacob was heartbroken. But God had not forgotten Joseph, and had a plan.",
 "Joseph's story takes up the last part of Genesis, and it begins with family jealousy at its worst. Favouritism and Joseph's dreams stir up hatred, and the brothers sell their own brother as a slave for twenty pieces of silver. Notice how the story looks on the surface: a boy abandoned, a father deceived. But the Bible keeps whispering, 'The Lord was with Joseph.' What looks like the end is the first step in God's rescue of the whole family from famine. Joseph's rejection and rise has reminded many Christians of Jesus, also betrayed for silver by one of his own.",
 ['Why were the brothers jealous?','How might Joseph have felt in the pit?','Can God use even bad things to bring about good?'],'Genesis 37:5',hue=330)

s('joseph2','Joseph Forgives His Brothers','OT',['Genesis 41:37-45','Genesis 45:1-15'],'🕊️',
 "In Egypt, Joseph worked hard and God was with him. In time, he became a leader of Egypt. A great hunger came, and Joseph's brothers came to Egypt to ask for food. They did not know him! At last Joseph said, 'I am your brother Joseph!' The brothers were afraid. But Joseph said, 'I forgive you. God sent me here to save many people.' They hugged and cried.",
 "In Egypt, Joseph was thrown into prison for something he did not do, but God was with him. He explained a dream for Pharaoh, which warned of seven good years and seven years of famine. Pharaoh made Joseph his chief helper, and Joseph stored up grain. When the famine came, Joseph's brothers travelled to Egypt to buy food. They bowed before him, not knowing who he was. At last Joseph cried out, 'I am Joseph! Is my father still alive?' The brothers were terrified. Joseph said, 'Do not be afraid. You meant to harm me, but God meant it for good.' He kissed them and wept, and the family was together again.",
 "Years after being sold, Joseph has power to take revenge, but he chooses forgiveness. He tests his brothers gently, and sees they have changed: Judah offers himself in place of Benjamin. Then Joseph breaks down and reveals himself. His words, 'You meant evil against me, but God meant it for good,' do not excuse what the brothers did. They show a man who sees God's bigger plan and offers mercy. Joseph is a sign of Christ, who forgives even those who rejected him and who 'feeds' the whole world with the bread of life.",
 ['How hard do you think it was for Joseph to forgive?','What changed in the brothers?','Who can you forgive this week?'],'Genesis 50:20',hue=48)

s('moses-baby','Baby Moses','OT',['Exodus 1:8-2:10'],'🧺',
 "In Egypt, the king was cruel to God's people, the Israelites. He wanted to hurt their baby boys. Moses' mother hid him as long as she could. Then she put him in a basket on the river. His sister Miriam watched. The king's daughter found him and loved him. She took him to be her son. Moses' own mother got to look after him too!",
 "The people of Israel had grown in number in Egypt, and the new Pharaoh, afraid of them, made them slaves and ordered that every Hebrew baby boy be thrown into the Nile. A mother hid her baby for three months. When she could hide him no longer, she made a little basket of reeds, coated it with tar, and set it among the reeds by the river. Miriam, his sister, stood at a distance to see what would happen. Pharaoh's daughter came to bathe, found the basket and felt sorry for the crying baby. Miriam stepped forward: 'Shall I find a nurse for you?' So the baby's own mother was paid to care for him! The princess named him Moses, meaning 'drawn out'.",
 "God's rescue of Israel begins with the courage of women: the midwives who refuse Pharaoh, a mother who risks everything and a sister who watches over a basket. The basket is like a small ark: the same word is used for Noah's boat. The child who will lead the people through the sea is first saved from water. God's plan is hidden in ordinary acts of love. Moses grows up in the palace but will always know where he comes from. Saint Stephen later praises how Moses was 'beautiful before God'.",
 ['What was brave about what Moses’ mother and sister did?','How did God use Pharaoh’s own daughter?','When might God be working in a hidden way in your life?'],'Exodus 2:10',hue=190)

s('burning-bush','Moses and the Burning Bush','OT',['Exodus 3:1-4:17'],'🔥',
 "Moses looked after sheep in the desert. One day he saw a bush on fire, but it did not burn up! God called, 'Moses! Take off your shoes. This is holy ground.' God said, 'I have seen my people's pain. I will send you to help them.' Moses was scared. 'I will be with you,' said God.",
 "Moses had left Egypt and become a shepherd. One day, on the mountain of Horeb, he saw a bush blazing with fire, but the bush did not burn up. As he stepped closer, a voice called, 'Moses, Moses! Take off your sandals, for this is holy ground.' It was God. 'I have seen the suffering of my people in Egypt, and I am sending you to bring them out.' Moses said, 'Who am I to do this?' and later, 'Who shall I say sent me?' God answered, 'I AM WHO I AM. Say, I AM has sent you.' God promised, 'I will be with you.'",
 "In the desert God reveals his name. 'I AM WHO I AM' tells us God is the One who simply is, always present, not a god among many but the source of everything. He is also personal: he has seen, heard and come down to rescue. Moses makes every excuse: I am no one, they will not believe me, I cannot speak well. God answers each excuse with 'I will be with you.' The bush that burns without being consumed has been, for Christians, a picture of Mary, who carried God's fire without being harmed, and of God's presence that does not destroy but gives life.",
 ['Why did God ask Moses to take off his sandals?','What excuses do we make when God asks something hard?','What does the name I AM tell us about God?'],'Exodus 3:14',hue=18)

s('passover','The Passover and the Plagues','OT',['Exodus 7:14-12:42'],'🚪',
 "Moses asked the king, 'Let God's people go!' But the king said no. So God sent signs, to show his power. At last, God told the families to put lamb's blood on their doors and share a special meal. That night God passed over their homes and kept them safe. In the morning the king said, 'Go!' This is called the Passover.",
 "Moses and his brother Aaron went to Pharaoh and said, 'The Lord says: Let my people go.' Pharaoh refused again and again, so God sent ten plagues on Egypt: the river turned to blood, then frogs, gnats and flies came, then more. Pharaoh's heart stayed hard. Before the last plague God told each Hebrew family to take a lamb without blemish, to mark their door frames with its blood and to eat a quick meal ready to travel. That night the angel of death passed over every marked door. Pharaoh finally let them go. Jews still keep Passover every year, and the Last Supper was a Passover meal.",
 "The plagues are not just a contest of power. Each strikes at something the Egyptians worshipped, showing that the Lord alone is God. When Pharaoh still refuses, God tells Israel to take a spotless lamb and mark the doorposts with its blood, to eat the meal standing, ready to go. The blood protects them. This 'Passover' becomes Israel's yearly memorial of their freedom. For Christians, it points straight to Jesus, 'the Lamb of God', whose blood sets us free, and to the Eucharist, in which the new Passover is made present at every Mass.",
 ['What did the lamb’s blood on the door mean?','Why do you think Pharaoh kept saying no?','How is the Mass like the Passover meal?'],'Exodus 12:13',hue=0)

s('red-sea','Crossing the Red Sea','OT',['Exodus 13:17-15:21'],'🌊',
 "God's people left Egypt. But the king changed his mind and chased them! The people were at the sea, and they were afraid. Moses said, 'Do not be afraid. God will save you.' God made a path through the sea. The people walked across on dry ground! When the king's army followed, the water closed back. The people sang and danced to thank God.",
 "The Israelites left Egypt, led by a tall cloud by day and a pillar of fire by night. But Pharaoh changed his mind and sent his army with chariots after them. The people were trapped between the army and the sea. They cried out in fear. Moses answered, 'Do not be afraid. Stand firm and watch the Lord rescue you today.' God told Moses to stretch out his staff. A strong wind blew all night and parted the sea, and the people walked through on dry land with walls of water on each side. When the Egyptians followed, the sea closed over them. Miriam took her tambourine and led the women in singing.",
 "This is the great event of the Old Testament: God makes a way where there is no way. The people had been slaves, and they walk free through the waters, and the pursuing army is swept away. Miriam and Moses sing the first song of the Bible, praising God as their strength and salvation. The early Christians saw in the sea a picture of Baptism: we pass through the water from slavery to sin into the freedom of God's children. That is why the story of the Exodus is read at the Easter Vigil every year.",
 ['How do you think the people felt standing at the sea?','Why did they sing?','What does the story teach us about trusting God when we feel trapped?'],'Exodus 14:14',hue=205)

s('manna','Bread from Heaven','OT',['Exodus 16:1-36'],'🍞',
 "In the desert, the people were hungry. They grumbled. God heard, and said, 'I will send bread from the sky.' In the morning the ground was covered with a flaky white food called manna. Each person took just what they needed for the day. God gave them what they needed, every day.",
 "A month after leaving Egypt, the people were in the desert, and their food ran out. They complained to Moses: 'In Egypt we had plenty of bread!' God said, 'I will rain bread from heaven for you.' Each morning, when the dew dried, there were fine flakes on the ground. The people said, 'What is it?' ('manna' in Hebrew). Moses told them, 'This is the bread the Lord has given you.' Each family gathered what they needed for the day, and no more. On the sixth day they gathered double, so they could rest on the Sabbath. For forty years God fed them.",
 "The people are free, but they quickly forget the God who rescued them, and grumble. God does not scold them harshly; he feeds them. Manna teaches daily trust: gather only enough for today, and what is kept overnight spoils, except before the Sabbath. Jesus recalls the manna in John 6, after feeding five thousand: 'Your ancestors ate the manna and died; I am the living bread that came down from heaven.' This is why Catholics see manna as a sign of the Eucharist, and why we pray, 'Give us this day our daily bread.'",
 ['What do you think manna tasted like?','Why could they only gather enough for one day?','How does Jesus give us bread from heaven?'],'Exodus 16:4',hue=52)

s('commandments','The Ten Commandments','OT',['Exodus 19:1-20:21'],'📜',
 "God called Moses up Mount Sinai. God gave his people ten rules, called the Ten Commandments. They help us to love God and to love others. God said, 'Love me most of all. Honour your mother and father. Do not steal. Tell the truth.' God gave us these rules because he loves us and wants us to be happy.",
 "Three months after leaving Egypt, the people came to Mount Sinai. The mountain shook with thunder and fire, and God spoke to Moses. 'I carried you out of Egypt on eagle's wings. Now, if you listen and keep my covenant, you will be my special people.' God gave the Ten Commandments: worship only me; do not misuse my name; keep the Sabbath; honour your father and mother; do not kill; be faithful in marriage; do not steal; do not lie; do not be greedy for what others have. They were written on two stone tablets. The first commandments are about loving God; the rest are about loving other people.",
 "The commandments begin with a reminder: 'I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of slavery.' They are not a list of arbitrary rules but the way free people live in a covenant with the God who freed them. The first three tell us how to love God, and the other seven how to love our neighbour. Jesus summed them up: love God with all your heart, and your neighbour as yourself. The Catechism spends a large part of its teaching on them, because they show us what love looks like in daily choices.",
 ['Which commandment is hardest for you? Why?','How do the commandments show love for God? For other people?','Why do you think God gave rules to people he had just set free?'],'Exodus 20:2-3',hue=270)
